\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Sources and the coupled comparison}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Sharatchandra, Thun and Weisz derive a rank-one site
projection and the reduced hop between selected spin lines \citet{Eqs. (2.26)–(2.34), pp. 210–211}{sharatchandrathunweisz1981susskind}.
Wingate gives the ordered spin product, its field change and
the sign from the preceding coordinates
\citet{Section 9.4, Eqs. (9.20)–(9.23), pp. 22–23}{wingate2025lattice}.
The edge calculation in \ref{fdip-014S} keeps each unitary
transport on the internal fiber.}

\p{Becher and Joos integrate forms over cells in
\citet{Section 3.1, Eqs. (3.1)–(3.4), pp. 352–353}{becher1982dirac}.
In their four-dimensional operator comparison, a fine site
is a coarse block corner plus the displacement for its
direction set. They transform the field, select a component,
assemble it into a cochain and obtain an intertwining identity
\citet{Section 3.3, Eqs. (3.41)–(3.46), p. 357}{becher1982dirac}.
Their raising and lowering operators enter with a relative
minus sign. For the counting-adjoint sum used here, the degree
phase in \ref{fdip-014X} gives #{2\ui} times the staggered
operator in every dimension.}

\p{The hypercube transform in \ref{fdip-00W9}
gives a free spin and flavor action containing a second
difference
\citet{Section 3, Eqs. (19)–(22), pp. 451–452}{klubergsternmorelnapolypetersson1983flavours}.
For [cochains with coefficient links](fdip-00SH), an operator
comparison on the fine torus would require specifying the
transports for the fine hops and proving the resulting
intertwining identity. The trivial-link comparison assigns
each based cell a fine site and a phase.}
